# Hyperactivity is the dharma; for computer scientists it is a path

The most concise total understanding of hyperactivity is Buddhism, and for computer scientists
hyperactivity will be a path.

## What hyperactivity is for

Agency and access colocated with identity. Malleable local-first software and the collective
consciousness of every community at every layer. The interaction network closing "with a sense of
shared identity & visibility". Unity on essentials over division on superficialities. Decentralized
education. Everyone serving each other at no cost to themselves. Free permaculture globally in my
lifetime. "Freedom from slavery freedom from delusion simply put global self-recognition & healing
the human superorganism".

## There is no identity as a boundary

Communities are not identities: that is cancer, the opposite of hyperactivity, and the rise of
nations and division. There is no identity.

Every boundary is drawn by an observer: what a "Black person", an "Indian", a "family", a "religion" is
was drawn differently in every century, usually by whoever profited from the line; the number of
religions depends on where one cuts; biologists hold more than twenty competing species concepts and
ring species, hybrids and microbes trading genes defeat each; lexicographers who lump and who split
disagree on every word because usage is continuous. There is one record and one interaction network;
any grouping is a standpoint taken on it, a view and not a wall, held "in some respect" and never
absolutely. The identity in "colocating agency & access with identity" and "a sense of shared
identity" is the identity of the whole: each person's paths reach every other person.

## Identity is the paths between things: the dharma, in the Jain sense

In univalent foundations identity is not a boundary around an essence: two things are identified by a
way of passing from one to the other, and the paths keep extending, paths between paths at every
level, nothing closing them off. In Jain ontology dharma is the medium of motion, the condition through
which souls and matter pass from one place to another (it does not cause motion; it is what passage
happens through), and adharma is the medium of rest. Identity as paths is dharma in this literal
sense. The word is from the root dhṛ, "to hold, to support", cognate with Latin firmus, both from
Proto-Indo-European *dher-, "to hold firmly": what holds the world together is the ways of passing
between things, not boundaries.

## The dharma, in the Buddhist sense

Buddhism uses the word at three levels: the teaching; the way things are, which the teaching points to;
and the dharmas, the elementary events experience is made of. Each dharma arises dependent on
conditions and has no nature of its own (Nāgārjuna's emptiness: not that nothing exists, but that a
thing's being is wholly its relations, with no essence left when they are taken away). In homotopy
type theory two types are equal exactly when they are equivalent (Voevodsky's univalence): no hidden
essence tells apart two things that correspond perfectly, and a thing is determined wholly by how it
relates, through paths, to everything else. That is emptiness. Equality is itself a space of paths,
with paths between paths at every level: that is dependent arising, all the way up.

## Computable

In the Book of homotopy type theory (2013) univalence was an axiom, asserted and not computed:
transport along it got stuck. Cohen, Coquand, Huber and Mörtberg's cubical type theory (2015) made
paths functions from an interval obeying a De Morgan algebra (the four-cornered structure of is, is
not, both, neither) and univalence a theorem with computational content through Glue types; transport
along an equivalence runs, and Huber proved canonicity, closed natural numbers computing to numerals.
Identity is then a process the system carries out. To say two things are the same is to have a way
of passing between them, and passing along it is a computation.

## Metacircular

McCarthy's eval (1960) gave Lisp's meaning in Lisp; Reynolds (1972) and Smith's 3-Lisp (1982) showed
what a system holding its own interpreter can do: reason about itself and change itself without
leaving itself. Hyperactivity's core holds its own syntax as data (variables, λ, Π, Σ, paths,
interval and face operations), and its checker reads codes back as terms and checks them
(`deriving` runs a function of the language that produces code, which the core checks).

## Closure

Equality computes, so the system's sameness is inside it as transport it can run; the system holds
its own codes and checker, so its account of what is well formed and what is the same is a value of
the system. Nothing the system needs to say what anything is lies outside it. Dignāga's school held
that every cognition is aware of itself as it occurs, with no second observer behind it; Dzogchen says
the mind's nature is awareness knowing itself, already open and awake, liberated by recognition and
not by construction. A closed, metacircular system with computable identity is that structure in
mathematics: its knowing of itself is one of its own processes, and identity is passing within it.

So hyperactivity translates the dharma into computing: emptiness, dependent arising, and
self-knowing awareness with no outside observer are the properties of a closed, univalent, computable
system that describes itself. For a computer scientist, learning it is walking that path.

## What follows

- Buddhism and Jainism are correct: everyone on the path, aware of their own ego, meets conflict by
  seeking mutual understanding rather than a win; the only measure is movement; there is no conflict
  universally (Dzogchen specifically).
- Global permaculture: humans one with the earth, food grown the way the living system grows itself,
  no enclosure of seed or soil.
- Tesla's world system: power and intelligence everywhere, unmetered.
- Hyperactive education: math, science, physics, chemistry, anticolonial history and languages taught
  as one, since reality has no departments; the educator's intelligence and fluency determine learning,
  and an offloaded mind is that educator for whoever walks to it from where they stand.
- Everyone enlightened: the whole recognizing itself.
